   "html_text": "The discrete but important influence of Schelling on Jaspers’s philosophy also provides a clue to understanding Jaspers’s philosophy of religion. At one level, Jaspers was philosophically committed to a sympathetic retrieval of religious contents. He was insistent that truth can only be interpreted as an element of radical alterity in reason, or as reason’s experience of its own limits. Similarly, he was insistent that the conditions of human freedom are not generated by human reason alone, but ",
